@* Table's Overview, written by hand after Button's: sorting and paging through the URL, previewed in
   each mode, then state in the component (Interactive only), row links and the look as code. Filtering,
   selection and loading data are topics of their own (Docs/Topics/Table*Topic). From docs/components.md
   (Table) and the dev host's TableDemo. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Topics</Heading>
<OverviewTopics Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Sorting and paging</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Sortable</Code> on a column and <Code>PageSize</Code> on the table. Headers and page numbers are links that set <Code>?sort=</Code> and <Code>?page=</Code>, so the table works with no script, survives a reload and can be shared.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-sort-page" />
<TextBlock><Code>InitialSort</Code> sets the order before anyone picks one (<Code>"-date"</Code> for newest first), and <Code>PageSizeOptions</Code> adds a "Rows per page" menu.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">State in the component</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>@@bind-Sort</Code>, <Code>@@bind-Page</Code> and <Code>@@bind-Filter</Code> on an interactive page to keep them in C# instead of the URL: for a table in a dialog, or anywhere the address shouldn't change.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-bound" />
<TextBlock>The table starts from the URL if it has values, and a new sort or filter goes back to page 1. Set the page back to 1 yourself when your own filters change. To set a filter from code, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/filtering#filters-in-code">Filters in code</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Row links</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>RowHref</Code> to make each row a link, and <Code>RowLabel</Code> to name it. Links, buttons and checkboxes inside the row still work on their own. Use <Code>RowCurrent</Code> to mark the row the page is showing, such as the one whose details are beside the table: it's drawn as selected and screen readers hear it as current (<Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell/aside#a-panel-for-the-chosen-row">AppShell</Anchor> has one with its details in an aside).</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@RowLinks</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Several tables on a page</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>QueryPrefix</Code> to give each table its own keys in the URL, so they sort and page on their own.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Prefix</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Lines, size and scrolling</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Size</Code>, <Code>Striped</Code>, <Code>ColumnLines</Code>, <Code>RowLines</Code>, <Code>ShadedHeader</Code> and <Code>Bordered</Code> for the look; turn <Code>Bordered</Code> off for a table in a card. <Code>MaxHeight</Code> scrolls the rows under a sticky header, and <Code>EmptyText</Code> shows when there are none.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Look</Code>

<Heading Level="2">On a phone</Heading>
<TextBlock>A table too wide for its space scrolls sideways. With <Code>SmallScreen="TableSmallScreen.Stack"</Code>, each row becomes a card of lines instead, a column's title beside its value, and the column titles stay in a row above, with their sort and filter buttons. It goes by the table's own width, worked out on the server from its columns' <Code>Width</Code> (8rem for one without), so it's right at the first paint with no script; set <Code>StackBelow</Code> to choose the width. For hiding columns instead, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/data-grid/adaptability">DataGrid</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-stacked" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

@KeyboardTable.Section(ApiComponent)

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<Heading Level="2">See also</Heading>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/data">Data and filters</Anchor>: the request, filters in code and as text, fields, and calling an API.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor>: search and filters of your own, in the URL.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/pagination">Pagination</Anchor>: page links for a list that isn't a table.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: state in the URL on static pages.</ListItem>
</List>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Table Items="orders" PageSize="10" Caption="Orders">
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Customer" Sortable Filterable />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Total" Format="C" Sortable />
            <TemplateColumn Title="Status">
                <Badge>@context.Status</Badge>
            </TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        """;

    private const string RowLinks = """
        <Table Items="orders" RowHref='o => $"orders/{o.Id}"' RowLabel='o => $"order {o.Number}"'>...</Table>
        """;

    private const string Prefix = """
        <Table Items="orders" QueryPrefix="o-" PageSize="10">...</Table>     @* ?o-sort=, ?o-page= *@
        <Table Items="refunds" QueryPrefix="r-" PageSize="10">...</Table>
        """;

    private const string Look = """
        <Table Items="orders" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Striped ColumnLines>...</Table>
        <Table Items="orders" MaxHeight="Length.Rem(20)" EmptyText="No orders yet">...</Table>
        """;
}
